  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: a8c78a3dc61624f33b78b8b42697355c, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::MuseumExhibit
  title: Modular Synthesizer
  meta: "Recovered from a recording studio  \xB7  Modular synthesizers spread in
    the 1960s"
  body: 'A synthesizer made sound from electricity instead of strings, reeds or skins.
    Oscillators created waves, filters shaped them, and patch cables connected the
    modules in endless combinations.


    When Robert Moog and others began selling
    modular systems in the 1960s, these machines moved from laboratories into studios.
    Electronic sound became the sound humans imagined for their own future, for robots
    and for space.'
  themeLink: The turntable stored human music; the synthesizer invented sounds that
    had never existed. Their technology did not only record their lives, it changed
    what those lives sounded like.
  credit: 'Model: "Gary''s Synthesizer" by Fredrik (kird3rf), CC BY 4.0, via Sketchfab.'
  image: {fileID: 0}
  interactPoint: {fileID: 0}
  interactRange: 2.8
  model: {fileID: 0}
  idleSpin: 10
  sway: 0
  swayAngle: 28
