--- !u!114 &572378689
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 572378687}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: a8c78a3dc61624f33b78b8b42697355c, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::MuseumExhibit
  title: Where Earth Was
  meta: "Viewing deck  \xB7  Voyager 1 photographed Earth from about 6 billion km
    on 14 February 1990"
  body: 'From here you can look back toward the humans'' home system: with your own
    eyes Earth is only a small blue light. Use the telescope on this deck to see
    it closer.


    In 1990, as Voyager 1 left the planets behind, its cameras turned
    around and photographed Earth as a single pale blue point, smaller than a pixel.
    The astronomer Carl Sagan called it a "pale blue dot": every human who ever lived,
    on one speck of light.'
  themeLink: The museum ends looking outward, the way the humans' last machines did.
    Everything in these rooms came from that dot.
  credit: 'Balcony and plaque built from Unity primitives. Fact source: NASA, Voyager
    1 "Pale Blue Dot" image (1990). Earth image: NASA Goddard Space Flight Center,
    Visible Earth (public domain). Photo: "The Blue Marble", Apollo 17 crew, NASA,
    7 December 1972 (public domain).'
  image: {fileID: 2800000, guid: 2debc24e2af7a4f0cb84905c6395d5bf, type: 3}
  interactPoint: {fileID: 0}
  interactRange: 2.6
  model: {fileID: 0}
  idleSpin: 10
  sway: 0
  swayAngle: 28
